[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning as you  may have noticed today is  national wear red day it's a day when  we're all encouraged to wear red to help  raise awareness for women's heart health  cardiovascular disease is the number one  killer of women in the u.s  the disease affects about 44 million an  alarming new survey by the cleveland  clinic found  coronavirus concerns are preventing some  heart disease patients from going to the  doctor  cbs news senior medical correspondent dr  tara narula joins us now she's a  cardiologist  and spokesperson for the american heart  association  tara good morning thank you anthony  she's wearing red is she wearing  pony's right we're all wearing it yes i  am tara  the numbers we just mentioned really are  alarming what what did women need to  know about this disease  well anthony the famous chinese military  leader wrote in the art of war  know your enemy and know yourself so one  word awareness  that is how we're going to win this  battle women need to understand  this is their biggest health threat so  if every woman whoever's listening this  morning put down your coffee stop what  you're doing and remember these three  things  cardiovascular disease kills one woman  every 80 seconds in this country  it kills more women than all forms of  cancer combined  and it is the leading cause of maternal  mortality  this is a disease that affects women of  all ages  all backgrounds so the same way that  it's ingrained in women to get her pap  smear  or her mammogram we need to start to  have women  understand that they need to know their  cardiovascular risk and have a  discussion with their doctor  starting early what are the risk factors  for women tara  well they're certainly the traditional  ones that you've heard about so  hypertension  high cholesterol obesity diabetes  smoking  alcohol family history but then there's  ones that many women don't often think  about so early menopause  migraine polycystic ovarian syndrome  lupus rheumatoid arthritis depression  anxiety  and finally a really important one  things that happen to a woman during  pregnancy so gestational hypertension or  diabetes  pre-term delivery pre-eclampsia all of  those things are going to raise her risk  so a woman should start being screened  at age 20.  she should know her numbers and by those  we mean blood pressure cholesterol body  mass index and blood sugar  and she should know her family history  boy tara you sort of answered the  question i was going to ask you what age  20 seems so young and does it matter if  you have  heart history bad heart history in your  family  i'm surprised by the and as twenty was  young  yeah i think what's so surprising to  people and what we try to educate them  about is that the seeds of  cardiovascular disease are planted so  early  and so much of it can be silent until  the day that you have your heart attack  or your stroke  but those that disease process is  starting at a very early age  tara why do you think so many women  ignore symptoms when when they get them  i want to know what are the symptoms  well so i can answer both symptoms  obviously most common  is chest pain pressure burning squeezing  but certainly women can also have  shortness of breath  decreased exercise tolerance fatigue  nausea lightheadedness passing out  jaw pain arm pain back pain all of those  if they're new  or they're concerning should signal a  woman to speak up and talk to her doctor  and as far as why they ignore them i  think they may not recognize  that this is could be signs or symptoms  of cardiovascular disease  but also women tend to not prioritize  their health particularly their heart  health and they downplay things they  prioritize their children  their work everything else so we need to  have women move themselves up to the top  of the list  but one other important thing anthony  and gail is that as a medical community  we also downplay we know that if a woman  arrives at the hospital she's going to  wait longer  than a man to be seen for chest pain  less often she's going to get aspirin  or guideline directed medical therapy or  diagnostic testing  this is a big problem i have a lot of  patients telling me my doctor told me  i'm just  anxious i'm just stressed out when in  fact they have a real problem  so awareness needs to happen on both  sides yeah and women need to keep  speaking up and pay attention to your  bodies thank you very much tara  always good to see you

